  "profile": {
    "video_id": "kenji:GDH6Xyzi_2Q",
    "purpose": "Shows how to make a simple Peruvian-style ceviche and explains how citrus acid 'cooks' raw fish, including how long to marinate it.",
    "video_type": "how_to",
    "thesis": "Acid denatures fish proteins much like heat does, without killing pathogens, so ceviche needs sushi-grade fish and has an ideal marination window. Under-marinated fish stays raw and slippery, and over-marinated fish turns dry, chalky and mushy.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Opens with a side-by-side comparison of fish marinated from about 2 minutes up to 2 hours.",
      "Ceviche is Peruvian and usually made with raw fish; versions elsewhere in South America and Mexico may use cooked seafood or tomato, ketchup or mayo bases (salsa rosada in Colombia).",
      "Use very fresh, firm, white-fleshed ocean fish of the kind you'd eat raw. Salmon also works. Trim connective tissue and pin bones.",
      "Cut the fish into roughly quarter- to half-inch chunks. Slices also work.",
      "This basic version is seasoned with red onion, serrano chili and cilantro, and ceviche is treated as a template for other flavours.",
      "Acid denatures proteins the way heat does, so the fish firms up and turns opaque, but it does not kill bacteria.",
      "Any citrus works: lime, lemon, sour orange, or a mix. The amount only needs to coat the fish (the recipe says about half a cup per pound).",
      "For firm fish, marinate at least 5 minutes, ideally about 15, and no more than 30 to 45.",
      "Served with boiled sweet potato, corn nuts in place of Peruvian fried corn, cilantro, olive oil and a habanero hot sauce.",
      "Ceviche keeps 'cooking' at the table, so eat it promptly."
    ],
